# Security & Secrets

## Secrets management

- Never commit secrets; use `.env` for local and CI secrets store for pipelines.
- Reference secrets via `${oc.env:VAR, ""}` in Hydra configs.
- Validate non-empty values in code before use.
- Treat the compiled/bundled artifacts (PyInstaller, Swift app, etc.) as secret-free: never embed keys in binaries or resource files.
- At runtime, inject secrets through environment variables only. On macOS, load them from Keychain (see `scripts/load-secrets-from-keychain.sh`) and export them when launching helper/backend processes; on CI/CD or production hosts use the platform’s secret store (GitHub Actions secrets, AWS SSM, etc.) to populate the process env before starting the app.
- Keep the injected values out of logs: mask when printing, and scrub process env after the child process terminates if possible.

References
- Config: [apps/backend/src/app/config.py](mdc:apps/backend/src/app/config.py)
- Hydra: [apps/backend/src/conf/config.yaml](mdc:apps/backend/src/conf/config.yaml)

## macOS app specifics

- Review entitlements when adding new capabilities (e.g., file access, networking).
- Avoid storing secrets in UserDefaults or logs; prefer Keychain if needed.
- Avoid logging sensitive filesystem paths or user data.
- When launching the backend from the helper, load `.env` first, then merge Keychain-derived secrets into the process environment right before spawning `uv run ...`; never persist the merged env to disk. Scripts under `scripts/*.sh` already support this workflow—reuse them instead of duplicating logic inside Swift.

## Backend specifics

- Avoid printing env variables in logs.
- Validate request inputs and enforce size limits on uploads.
- Prefer least-privileged file system access patterns.

## Filesystem & Permissions

- Ensure `VOYAGER_LOG_FILE` directory exists and is writable.
- Avoid running backend with elevated privileges on dev machines.

## Logs & PII

- Follow [05-logging-observability.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/05-logging-observability.mdc) for detailed logging guidelines.
- Redact PII and tokens; do not log raw payloads.
- Rotate or truncate large logs to reduce exposure duration.

